        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          OBJECTIVES:<br>
The hypothesis of this investigation prescribes that passive intermittent artificial gravity (AG) training will increase orthostatic tolerance. Modification of autonomic balance and baroreflex activity may or may not be associated with artificial gravity training. 
<P>
It is expected that increased orthostatic tolerance will be a result of  training-induced  increases in mean values of cardiac output (CO), heart rate (HR) and spectral power of arterial pressure (AP), decreased mean AP and TPR and no major change in sympathetic/parasympathetic balance as indicated by spectral analysis, cross-correlation of AP and HR, or catecholamines.  
<P>
APPROACH:<br>
R–R intervals and systolic blood pressure (SBP) were re-sampled at 4 Hz using a cubic spline interpolation, and the linear trend was removed before spectral power calculation. Power spectral densities (PSD) were calculated using Welch’s method of modified periodograms using segments overlapping by 50% and a 1024-point FFT. The PSD was integrated using the trapezoidal method over the specific frequency range to determine the low frequency (LF) (0.04–0.15 Hz) and high frequency (HF) (0.15–0.40 Hz) power. Normalized high frequency (HFnu) was calculated as HF divided by the sum of LF and HF. Spectral power was calculated for the first 5.5 min of rest and the semi steady-state period of tilt, excluding any sudden increases in heart rate or decreases in blood pressure (BP) associated with the beginning and end of tilt. For all subjects, this semi steady-state period excluded the first 30 seconds of upright tilt, to avoid a low frequency bias caused by the transient rise in heart rate associated with change in posture. For the same reason, for presyncopal subjects, approximately the last 30 seconds of upright data, were excluded if the blood pressure was falling. <P>
RESULTS:<br>
There were no effects of bed rest or AG training on high frequency systolic blood pressure (HF SBP) in either the pre-tilt or head up tilt (HUT) condition. Bed rest increased pre-tilt levels of low frequency systolic blood pressure (LF SBP) and HUT levels of HFnu RRi and decreased pre-tilt levels of HFnu RRi. There was a significant group by day interaction in HUT LF SBP spectral power (delta LF SBP).  The control subjects decreased delta LF SBP and treatment subjects increased delta LF SBP after bed rest. <end>
        </div>

